---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ReportForm from '../components/ReportForm.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';

const serviceSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  '@id': `${SITE.url}/service#service`,
  name: 'Answer engine optimisation',
  serviceType: 'Answer engine optimisation',
  description:
    'Answer surface measurement, diagnosis, entity and content work, third-party placement, and re-measurement against a control set.',
  provider: { '@id': `${SITE.url}/#organization` },
  areaServed: SITE.areaServed.map((c) => ({ '@type': 'Country', identifier: c })),
  url: `${SITE.url}/service`,
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Service"
  description="How a Brandure engagement runs: what gets audited across four answer engines, what gets delivered, and what a client actually receives at each stage."
  schemas={[serviceSchema]}
>
  <p class="eyebrow">Service</p>
  <h1>What the work actually is</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    Every engagement starts with a measurement, because without a baseline
    there is no way to show that anything done afterwards worked.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Baseline</h2>
  <p>
    We agree a prompt set with you — the questions a buyer types when they are
    choosing, not researching. Market is agreed explicitly rather than assumed:
    adding a city to a query your buyers never localise produces a different
    test, not a sharper one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each prompt runs across ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini and Google, from clean
    sessions with personalisation off. A signed-in run measures your own search
    history rather than the market.
  </p>
  <p><strong>You receive:</strong> the frozen prompt set, and a table of who was named on each engine, in what order, with the domains each answer cited.</p>

  <h2>2. Diagnosis</h2>
  <p>
    Absence has causes and they are not interchangeable. A model may fail to
    resolve your brand as an entity — it does not know what you are. It may be
    grounding the category on third-party sources you have no presence in. Or
    it may have a settled preference that content alone will not shift.
  </p>
  <p><strong>You receive:</strong> which of those applies per engine, and what follows from it.</p>

  <h2>3. Execution</h2>
  <p>
    Entity and structured-data work on your own properties, so a model can
    resolve who you are and what you sell. Content built to be lifted into an
    answer — direct claims, clean structure, explicit comparisons. And
    placement into the third-party sources the models actually draw on.
  </p>
  <p>
    That last one usually carries the most weight and is the least comfortable,
    because it is earned rather than published. Most citations in AI answers
    come from sources a brand does not own, which is why placement is core to
    the work rather than an add-on.
  </p>
  <p><strong>You receive:</strong> the changes themselves, plus a placement plan naming target sources in priority order.</p>

  <h2>4. Re-measurement</h2>
  <p>
    Against the identical frozen prompt set, alongside a control set of prompts
    we deliberately leave untouched for the length of the engagement.
  </p>
  <p>
    Answer engines move on their own — models update, competitors act, indexes
    refresh. The control set is what separates our effect from theirs. Without
    it you have a number that went up and no way to attribute it, which is not
    evidence.
  </p>
  <p><strong>You receive:</strong> before and after on both sets, with the divergence between them stated plainly, and the raw counts behind every figure.</p>

  <h2>What we will tell you</h2>
  <p>
    If the baseline shows a category already settled on two incumbents with no
    visible route in, we will say so and decline the work. A measurement that
    only ever produces a reason to hire us is not a measurement.
  </p>

  <ReportForm id="report" heading="Start with the free report" />
</BaseLayout>
